<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Supreme Court of the United States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as SCOTUS</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Supreme Court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> is the highest court of the federal judiciary, created under Article Three of the Constitution and organised by the Judiciary Act of 1789. It has final appellate jurisdiction over federal cases and over state cases that turn on constitutional or federal law. In 1803 it asserted the power of judicial review, the ability to strike down a statute that violates the Constitution. Nine justices, the chief justice and eight associate justices, sit for life, appointed by the president with the advice and consent of the Senate. It receives about 7,000 petitions a year and grants about 80.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/supreme-court-of-the-united-states.jpg" alt="Supreme Court of the United States" width="330" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UpstateNYer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USSupremeCourtWestFacade.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>At the Constitutional Convention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1787, delegates debated how to separate the powers of the legislature and executive and how to set up a national judiciary. In the British tradition, judicial matters had been the responsibility of the royal, executive authority. Delegates wary of a strong central government argued that state courts could enforce national laws, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> and others wanted a national judicial authority chosen by the national legislature. The Constitution does not fix the size of the court. The first Congress set up a court of six members through the Judiciary Act of 1789, which also created a quorum of four.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The court first met on 1 February 1790 at the Merchants' Exchange Building in New York City, and in Washington it used rooms in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> until 1935, when it moved into a building designed by Cass Gilbert. Under its first chief justices it heard few cases, and its most prominent early case, Chisholm v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> (1793), was reversed within two years by the Eleventh Amendment. Justices rode circuit under the act of 1789, a practice many opposed; it ended in 1891 and was formally abolished in 1911.</p><p>In 1803 the court asserted the power of judicial review, which the Constitution nowhere mentions. Its power to hear appeals from state courts was upheld in Martin v. Hunter's Lessee (1816) and Cohens v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> (1821). The Taney Court, from 1836 to 1864, is remembered chiefly for Dred Scott v. Sandford, which helped precipitate the Civil War. Its size was last changed in 1869, when it was set at nine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The court has a chief justice and eight associate justices, who hold office for life unless they die, retire, resign or are impeached and removed. The president appoints them with the advice and consent of the Senate, and the Constitution sets no qualifications. Nearly all cases arrive by petition for a writ of certiorari, of which about 7,000 are filed each year and about 80 granted. A term begins on the first Monday of October and runs until June or early July. Each side usually has thirty minutes of oral argument, and the senior justice in the majority, always the chief justice if in it, assigns the opinion. The court cannot enforce its rulings itself.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Supreme Court of the United States” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Cuban revolution of 1959, emigrants carried Santería to Miami, New York and beyond, and in 1993 the <b>Supreme Court of the United States</b> upheld the right of a Santería church in Florida to practise animal sacrifice.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Justice Louis Brandeis of the <b>Supreme Court of the United States</b> wrote in 1932 that “a single courageous State may, if its citizens choose, serve as a laboratory; and try novel social and economic experiments without risk to the rest of the country.”</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
